# AWS Global Infrastructure — High Availability & Fault Tolerance

## Core Idea
AWS designs its global infrastructure to ensure **high availability** and **fault tolerance**, so applications remain accessible even when failures occur.

## Business Analogy (Coffee Shop)
- A single coffee shop failure (power outage, equipment damage) halts business.
- A **chain of shops** ensures customers are still served elsewhere.
- Result: Business continuity despite local failures.

## Why a Single Data Center Is Risky
- One data center = single point of failure.
- Power outages, network cuts, or natural disasters can bring everything down.
- Modern applications require resilience by design.

## High Availability (HA)
- Ensures applications stay accessible with **minimal downtime**.
- If one component fails, another immediately takes over.
- Focus: **continuity of service**.

## Fault Tolerance (FT)
- Goes beyond HA by allowing systems to function even if **multiple components fail**.
- Resilience is built into every layer.
- Goal: **no single failure should break the system**.

## AWS Global Design
AWS achieves HA and FT through geographic distribution.

### Regions
- Separate geographic locations around the world.
- Examples: Paris, Tokyo, São Paulo, Dublin, Ohio.
- Designed to be close to customers to reduce latency.

### Availability Zones (AZs)
- Each Region contains **3 or more AZs**.
- AZs are physically separated to avoid shared failures.
- Each AZ has independent power, networking, and connectivity.

### Data Centers
- AZs contain one or more discrete data centers.
- Built with redundancy at every infrastructure layer.

## Multi-Region Strategy
- Critical workloads often run across **multiple Regions**.
- If one Region fails, traffic can fail over to another.
- Enables disaster recovery at a global scale.

## Key Takeaways
- High availability keeps services running during failures.
- Fault tolerance prevents cascading outages.
- Regions and AZs are the backbone of AWS reliability.
- Designing for failure is a **best practice**, not an edge case.

## Bottom Line
AWS’s global infrastructure ensures your applications stay online—even when disasters happen or lattes spill.
